	<article class="cask-content" aria-label="The Cask of Amontillado study sections">
		<ContentSection id="author" title="Author" summary="Edgar Allan Poe (1809–1849). This story was written during a prolific period of his career refining his theory of the short story." align="left">
			<p>Edgar Allan Poe (1809–1849). This story was written a few years before "Annabel Lee" (1846 vs. 1849), during the same late, prolific period of his career when he was refining his theory of the short story: that every sentence should serve a single, unified emotional effect (in this case, dread and psychological horror rather than grief).</p>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="context" title="Time, Era, and Societal Context" summary="Poe deliberately sets the story in the past and abroad during Carnival season, a common Gothic technique." align="right">
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Setting:</strong> An unnamed Italian city during Carnival season. Poe deliberately sets it in the past and abroad, a common Gothic technique that let 19th-century American writers explore violence and moral decay at a "safe" historical and cultural distance.</li>
				<li><strong>Carnival culture:</strong> Real Italian carnivals involved masks, costumes, confetti, heavy drinking, and general chaos — the perfect cover for Montresor to lead Fortunato away unnoticed.</li>
				<li><strong>Secret societies:</strong> The story includes a brief exchange about Freemasonry. American suspicion of the Masons peaked in the late 1820s and 1830s, but by 1846 it was fading. Poe uses it as dramatic irony (Fortunato assumes Montresor is a fellow Mason; Montresor's real "membership" is as a stonemason, since he's about to wall Fortunato in).</li>
				<li><strong>Poe's own life:</strong> Written during a period of professional rivalries and personal loss. The leading Poe editor, Thomas Ollive Mabbott, notes that a bitter 1846 quarrel between Poe and the cohorts of Thomas Dunn English and Hiram Fuller likely fueled the story's cold cruelty.</li>
				<li><strong>Real-life inspiration:</strong> Mabbott notes that Poe's immediate source for the walled-up victim was likely "A Man Built in a Wall" by Joel T. Headley, a traveler's account of a skeleton found inside an Italian church wall.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="summary" title="Summary (Characters, Setting, Plot)" summary="Montresor lures his enemy Fortunato into the catacombs with the promise of rare wine, and walls him up alive." align="left">
			<p><strong>Characters:</strong></p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Montresor:</strong> The narrator. A proud nobleman driven to murder by a series of "injuries" and one final "insult" from Fortunato (never specified). Calm, calculating, and remorseless.</li>
				<li><strong>Fortunato:</strong> Montresor's acquaintance, a wealthy wine connoisseur with one major weakness: pride. Ironically, his name means "the fortunate one."</li>
				<li><strong>Luchesi:</strong> A rival wine expert mentioned but never seen; Montresor uses him as a psychological trigger to make Fortunato insist on going to taste the wine himself.</li>
			</ul>
			<p><strong>Setting:</strong> An unnamed Italian city during Carnival, moving from the noisy streets down into the cold, damp catacombs beneath the Montresor family palace.</p>
			<p><strong>Plot:</strong> Montresor tells us he has vowed revenge on Fortunato for an unspecified insult. During Carnival, he approaches the already-drunk Fortunato and tells him he has acquired a cask of rare Amontillado sherry but is unsure if it's genuine — and mentions he was about to ask Luchesi to verify it instead. Fortunato's pride is provoked; he insists on tasting it himself. Montresor leads him down into the family catacombs, plying him with more wine along the way. At a small crypt, Montresor suddenly chains Fortunato to the wall and begins bricking him into a niche, ignoring his cries and pleas. Fifty years later, Montresor tells us, no one has disturbed the bones. "In pace requiescat" — may he rest in peace.</p>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="analysis" title="Analysis (Salient Points)" summary="Poe builds the story around unity of effect, driving toward one feeling: creeping dread, narrated by an unreliable narrator." align="right">
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Unity of effect:</strong> Nothing is wasted; every detail (the carnival, the wine, the descent, the coat of arms) drives toward one feeling: creeping dread.</li>
				<li><strong>Unreliable narrator:</strong> The story is narrated entirely by Montresor, fifty years after the fact. We never learn what the "thousand injuries" or the final "insult" actually were, forcing the reader to question whether Montresor's grievance is exaggerated. (Mabbott even hints that the mention of "the lady Fortunato" suggests a woman may have been involved).</li>
				<li><strong>Dramatic irony:</strong> We know Montresor wants revenge from the first line, but not how he'll carry it out. Fortunato remains completely unaware, so every friendly toast reads as savage mockery.</li>
				<li><strong>The ending:</strong> The ending complicates any easy sense of triumph. Montresor insists true revenge should not "overtake" the avenger, yet the fact that he can recall every detail fifty years later suggests the murder never actually gave him peace. Mabbott argues this makes the story perhaps the most moral of Poe's tales: the murderer at the end remembers that his victim rests in peace, something the criminal has been unable to do for fifty years.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="themes" title="Major Themes and Motifs" summary="Revenge, pride, deception, and descent into darkness are the major themes." align="left">
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Revenge and its true cost:</strong> Montresor's opening vow; his claim that revenge must go unpunished and be known to the victim; his still-vivid memory 50 years later.</li>
				<li><strong>Pride as a fatal weakness:</strong> Fortunato's repeated boasting about his wine expertise, which Montresor weaponizes.</li>
				<li><strong>Deception / duplicity:</strong> Montresor's friendly manner masking murderous intent throughout; the literal and figurative masks of Carnival.</li>
				<li><strong>Descent into darkness:</strong> The literal descent from the bright carnival street into the dark, damp catacombs mirrors Montresor's descent into cold-blooded murder.</li>
				<li><strong>Alcohol as motif:</strong> Wine appears repeatedly (Amontillado, Médoc, De Grâve) — it loosens Fortunato's guard and underscores his lack of self-control.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="symbols" title="Major Symbols" summary="The jester costume, the coat of arms, the catacombs, and the trowel are key symbols." align="right">
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Fortunato's jester costume:</strong> Marks him as "the fool" of the story — someone easily manipulated, whose good fortune is about to be reversed.</li>
				<li><strong>Montresor's coat of arms (a golden foot crushing a serpent that is biting the heel) and family motto, "Nemo me impune lacessit" ("No one attacks me with impunity"):</strong> Symbolizes Montresor's family code of vengeance — but ironically, the serpent still bites the heel even as it's crushed.</li>
				<li><strong>The catacombs / niter-crusted walls:</strong> Symbolize both literal death (ancestral bones) and the buried, decaying state of Montresor's own conscience.</li>
				<li><strong>The trowel:</strong> A symbol of Montresor's true, hidden identity — not a Mason in the fraternal sense, but literally the "mason" who will wall Fortunato up.</li>
				<li><strong>The carnival mask:</strong> A symbol of concealment and duplicity — the surface gaiety of the festival hiding Montresor's true, murderous purpose.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="conventions" title="Important Conventions" summary="The story utilizes classical unities, Gothic elements, and dramatic irony." align="left">
			<p>"The Cask of Amontillado" is the work where the drama conventions fit best.</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Classical unities (loosely kept):</strong> The time is one evening during Carnival. The place is mostly the Montresor vaults. The action is one plot, the revenge.</li>
				<li><strong>Noble birth:</strong> Montresor comes from an old family with a coat of arms and a motto. He tells Fortunato, "The Montresors were a great and numerous family."</li>
				<li><strong>Tragic flaw:</strong> Fortunato's pride in his wine expertise is what leads him to his death.</li>
				<li><strong>Free will:</strong> Fortunato keeps choosing to go deeper, even when Montresor offers (falsely) to turn back.</li>
				<li><strong>Gothic:</strong> It has catacombs, bones, darkness, a decaying aristocratic family, and live burial.</li>
				<li><strong>Unity of effect:</strong> This is Poe's short story rule: every detail builds toward one single feeling, which here is dread.</li>
				<li><strong>First-person confessional narrator:</strong> The murderer tells his own story fifty years later.</li>
				<li><strong>Dramatic irony:</strong> The reader knows more than Fortunato does.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="resonance" title="How the Text Resonates Today" summary="The core idea that an unaddressed grudge can calcify into obsession still describes real interpersonal conflicts." align="right">
			<ul>
				<li>The core idea — that an unaddressed grudge can quietly calcify into obsession — still describes real patterns in interpersonal conflict, workplace resentment, and larger-scale conflicts driven by a desire for retribution.</li>
				<li>Modern readers often connect the story's central tension (cold, calculated cruelty hidden behind a friendly face) to present-day discussions of manipulation and duplicity in relationships.</li>
				<li>Fortunato's downfall through vanity and competitiveness (his rivalry with Luchesi) still mirrors how pride and ego can be turned into an easy weakness for others to exploit.</li>
			</ul>
		</ContentSection>

		<ContentSection id="references" title="References and Conclusion" summary="Scholarly sources including Mabbott's collected works and Baraban's literary analysis." align="left">
			<p><strong>References:</strong></p>
			<ul>
				<li>Baraban, E. V. (2004). The motive for murder in “The Cask of Amontillado” by Edgar Allan Poe. Rocky Mountain Review of Language and Literature, 58(2), 47–62.</li>
				<li>Mabbott, T. O. (Ed.). (1978). Collected works of Edgar Allan Poe: Vol. 3. Tales and sketches. Belknap Press of Harvard University Press.</li>
				<li>SuperSummary. (n.d.). The Cask of Amontillado themes.</li>
			</ul>
			<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> "The Cask of Amontillado" is Poe's coldest story precisely because it refuses to give us a satisfying moral. Montresor "wins," but Poe leaves enough cracks in his fifty-years-later confession to suggest that unpunished revenge may still be its own kind of prison.</p>
		</ContentSection>
	</article>
